    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A league of the Mohawk, Oneida, Onondaga, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cayuga-people">Cayuga</a> and Seneca, joined by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuscarora-people">Tuscarora</a> around 1722, under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-law-of-peace">Great Law of Peace</a>.</li><li>Fifty hereditary chiefs, chosen and removable by clan mothers, decided by consensus in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-council-qing">Grand Council</a> at Onondaga.</li><li>Strongest Native power of the north-east for over a century, it was split by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolution">American Revolution</a> but its councils still meet.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Haudenosaunee issue their own passports, and their national lacrosse team has competed internationally under the confederacy’s flag, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiawatha-belt">Hiawatha Belt</a>, in a sport they helped to create.</p></aside>
    </section>

