    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hiawatha"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hiawatha.jpg" alt="Hiawatha" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hiawatha">Hiawatha</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hiawatha_-_Eakins.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Thomas Eakins, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Haudenosaunee, the People of the Longhouse, were Iroquoian-speaking farming peoples of what is now upstate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, living in palisaded villages of bark longhouses between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudson-river">Hudson River</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genesee-river">Genesee</a>. According to Haudenosaunee tradition, a time of blood feuds and endless revenge among them was ended by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-peacemaker">the Peacemaker</a>, Deganawida, a Huron-born prophet, and his spokesman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiawatha">Hiawatha</a>, an Onondaga who had lost his family to violence. Together they persuaded the Mohawk, Oneida, Onondaga, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cayuga-people">Cayuga</a> and Seneca to accept the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-law-of-peace">Great Law of Peace</a>, the <em>Gayanashagowa</em>, and finally won over the feared Onondaga chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tadodaho">Tadodaho</a>, whose name became the title of the league’s presiding chief. The five nations buried their weapons beneath a Great Tree of Peace, a white pine, and became one symbolic longhouse stretching across their lands, with the Mohawk as keepers of the eastern door and the Seneca of the western door. The date of the founding is uncertain and much debated. Proposals range from the twelfth century, partly based on a tradition linking it to a solar eclipse, to the late sixteenth century, and archaeology suggests a gradual consolidation of villages and alliances over the fifteenth and sixteenth centuries. The register follows a mid-fifteenth-century date, and the league was certainly in existence when Europeans first met its peoples in the early seventeenth century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/albany-new-york"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/albany-new-york.jpg" alt="Albany, New York" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/albany-new-york">Albany, New York</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Albany,_New_York.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Quintin Soloviev, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Haudenosaunee society was matrilineal. People belonged to the clan of their mother, such as Turtle, Wolf or Bear, and extended families of related women lived together in longhouses that could reach more than 50 metres in length, each housing several families around shared hearths. Women owned and worked the fields, growing the Three Sisters, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, beans and squash, which provided most of the diet, while men hunted, fished, traded, made war and conducted diplomacy. Villages of several hundred to a few thousand people moved every decade or two as soils and firewood were exhausted. Before the great epidemics the five nations may have numbered around 20,000 people or more, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> and other diseases, notably in 1634, killed perhaps half of them. In the seventeenth century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">fur trade</a> transformed the economy. The Mohawk traded beaver pelts with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-orange">Fort Orange</a>, founded in 1624 on the site of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albany-new-york">Albany</a>, for metal tools, cloth and firearms, and later traded with the English. Wampum, strings and belts of white and purple shell <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a>, recorded agreements, conveyed messages and gave weight to words in council. Religious life centred on thanksgiving to the Creator and the forces of nature through seasonal ceremonies, and in 1799 the Seneca prophet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/handsome-lake">Handsome Lake</a> founded the Longhouse religion, which is still practised today.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lake-george"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-lake-george.jpg" alt="Battle of Lake George" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lake-george">Battle of Lake George</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Johnson_saving_Dieskau.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Benjamin West, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Haudenosaunee warfare was originally driven by the mourning war, in which raiding parties took captives to replace the dead, to be adopted into families or executed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">fur trade</a>, epidemics and access to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> guns transformed this practice in the seventeenth century. The hostility of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a> began when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-de-champlain">Samuel de Champlain</a> and his Huron and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algonquin-people">Algonquin</a> allies killed several Mohawk leaders near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-champlain">Lake Champlain</a> in 1609. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beaver-wars">Beaver Wars</a> the league fought for control of furs and captives: in 1649 Haudenosaunee war parties destroyed the main towns of the Huron Confederacy and dispersed its people, then broke the Neutral Confederacy around 1651 and the Erie Nation by about 1656, absorbing thousands of captives. French counter-attacks were severe. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marquis-de-tracy">Marquis de Tracy</a> burned Mohawk villages in 1666, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacques-rene-de-brisay-de-denonville">Marquis de Denonville</a> invaded Seneca country in 1687, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-de-buade-de-frontenac">Frontenac</a> struck the Onondaga in 1696, while Haudenosaunee warriors raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lachine-massacre">Lachine</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shobak-castle">Montreal</a> in 1689. Exhausted by these wars, the league made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-peace-of-montreal">Great Peace of Montreal</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and its Native allies in 1701. In the eighteenth century Mohawk warriors under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hendrick-theyanoguin">Hendrick Theyanoguin</a> fought beside the British at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lake-george">Battle of Lake George</a> in 1755, and in the American Revolution the league’s nations fought one another, notably at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-oriskany">Battle of Oriskany</a> in 1777.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tadodaho"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tadodaho.jpg" alt="Tadodaho" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tadodaho">Tadodaho</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:22dec06-COP15-opening-ceremony-5714_(52548203403).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">UN Biodiversity, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Great Law created a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-council-qing">Grand Council</a> of fifty hereditary chiefs, the <em>hoyaneh</em>, whose titles were distributed unequally among the nations: fourteen for the Onondaga, ten for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cayuga-people">Cayuga</a>, nine each for the Mohawk and Oneida, and eight for the Seneca. Numbers did not decide votes, because each nation spoke with one voice and decisions required consensus. The Onondaga kept the central fire and the wampum belts that recorded the league’s laws and agreements, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tadodaho">Tadodaho</a> presided over the council. Debate followed a set procedure: the Mohawk and Seneca, as elder brothers, considered a matter first, then passed it across the fire to the Oneida and Cayuga, the younger brothers, before the Onondaga confirmed or returned the decision. Clan mothers, the senior women of each lineage, chose the chiefs from eligible men, advised them, and could remove those who failed in their duties. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/condolence-ceremony">Condolence Ceremony</a> mourned dead chiefs and installed their successors. The confederacy was a framework of peace among its members rather than a centralised government: each nation kept wide autonomy and could pursue its own diplomacy and war, which sometimes produced divided policies. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuscarora-people">Tuscarora</a> moved north from North Carolina after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuscarora-war">Tuscarora War</a> of 1711–1713 and were adopted around 1722, they joined under the sponsorship of the Oneida, and the league became the Six Nations.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-the-united-states"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/constitution-of-the-united-states.jpg" alt="Constitution of the United States" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-the-united-states">Constitution of the United States</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Constitution_of_the_United_States_-_DPLA_-_9ca804144bd5965e992ae3528bc3c6a3_(page_1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Constitutional Convention, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For more than a century the Haudenosaunee held the balance of power in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a> and the English colonies. Through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/covenant-chain">Covenant Chain</a>, the alliance made at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albany-new-york">Albany</a> in 1677 with Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edmund-andros">Edmund Andros</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> and later extended to other colonies, they became the principal Native partner of the English and claimed authority over many dependent peoples, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lenape">Delaware</a> and remnant groups of the Susquehannock. After 1701 their policy of neutrality between the empires allowed them to play the two powers against each other. Their councils, oratory and wampum diplomacy set the forms of treaty-making that colonial officials adopted, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-johnson">Sir William Johnson</a>, British superintendent of Indian Affairs, built his influence on close ties with the Mohawk. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benjamin-franklin">Benjamin Franklin</a> pointed to the union of the Six Nations when advocating colonial union, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albany-congress">Albany Congress</a> of 1754 met partly to renew the alliance with them. Whether the league directly influenced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-the-united-states">United States Constitution</a> is debated by historians, although the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-congress">United States Congress</a> acknowledged such an influence in a resolution of 1988. Haudenosaunee communities today remain among the most active Indigenous nations in asserting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>, issuing their own passports and fielding national lacrosse teams in international competition.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Into the Present</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/american-revolution"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/american-revolution.jpg" alt="American Revolution" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/american-revolution">American Revolution</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Declaration_of_Independence_(1819),_by_John_Trumbull.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">John Trumbull, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolution">American Revolution</a> broke the league’s unity. In 1777 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-council-qing">Grand Council</a> could not agree on a common policy and covered its central fire at Onondaga, leaving each nation to choose its side. Most Mohawk, Onondaga, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cayuga-people">Cayuga</a> and Seneca joined the British, led in part by the Mohawk war leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-brant">Joseph Brant</a>, while most Oneida and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuscarora-people">Tuscarora</a> supported the Americans. In 1779 the Sullivan Expedition destroyed some forty Haudenosaunee towns and their crops, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1783">Treaty of Paris</a> of 1783 made no provision for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>’s Native allies. The Treaty of Fort Stanwix of 1784 and later state purchases stripped the nations of most of their lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, although the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-canandaigua">Treaty of Canandaigua</a> of 1794 recognised the remaining lands of the Six Nations and is still honoured with an annual distribution of cloth. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haldimand-proclamation">Haldimand Proclamation</a> of 1784, Brant led many loyalist Haudenosaunee to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-river-ontario">Grand River</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a>, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/six-nations-of-the-grand-river">Six Nations of the Grand River</a> is now the most populous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-nations-in-canada">First Nations</a> reserve in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>. In 1924 Canada imposed an elected band council at Six Nations, after the Cayuga chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deskaheh">Deskaheh</a> had sought recognition from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a>, but the traditional chiefs continue to meet. The Grand Council still sits at Onondaga, near Syracuse, New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york-maine">York</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Iroquois Confederacy, or Haudenosaunee, united five and later six nations under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-law-of-peace">Great Law of Peace</a>, governed by consensus among hereditary chiefs chosen by clan mothers, and for more than a century held the balance of power between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>; its councils still meet today.</em></p>

